1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,139
Ask Penny how much she spends on subscriptions, and she'll guess about eighty-six dollars a month.

2
00:00:06,459 --> 00:00:09,660
In one survey, that's exactly what people guessed.

3
00:00:09,980 --> 00:00:14,660
When they actually added it up, the real number was two hundred and nineteen.

4
00:00:14,980 --> 00:00:16,980
More than twice as much.

5
00:00:17,629 --> 00:00:20,750
Last episode, free apps sold Penny's time.

6
00:00:21,070 --> 00:00:25,480
Subscriptions go straight for her wallet, quietly, every single month.

7
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:29,769
Here are the three engines that make the subscription trap work.

8
00:00:30,420 --> 00:00:34,070
That gap is a hundred and thirty-three dollars a month.

9
00:00:34,390 --> 00:00:39,490
Over a year, that's almost sixteen hundred dollars Penny didn't know she was spending.

10
00:00:39,810 --> 00:00:44,710
And the companies on the other side of that gap know exactly what they're doing.

11
00:00:45,359 --> 00:00:47,090
Engine number one:

12
00:00:47,409 --> 00:00:48,689
the free trial.

13
00:00:49,009 --> 00:00:50,820
Free sounds harmless.

14
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:54,810
But to start it, Penny has to type in her card details.

15
00:00:55,130 --> 00:00:56,609
That's the whole trick.

16
00:00:56,929 --> 00:01:00,100
The company isn't betting she'll love the product.

17
00:01:00,420 --> 00:01:02,600
It's betting she'll forget the date.

18
00:01:03,250 --> 00:01:05,730
When the trial ends, it doesn't ask.

19
00:01:06,049 --> 00:01:08,519
It just converts into a paid plan.

20
00:01:08,840 --> 00:01:11,760
Nothing happens that Penny has to notice.

21
00:01:12,079 --> 00:01:13,849
No new box arrives.

22
00:01:14,170 --> 00:01:16,030
No reminder pops up.

23
00:01:16,349 --> 00:01:20,150
Just one more line on a statement most people never read.

24
00:01:20,799 --> 00:01:22,549
Engine number two:

25
00:01:22,870 --> 00:01:27,000
forgetting. Each subscription is small on its own.

26
00:01:27,319 --> 00:01:28,859
Five dollars here.

27
00:01:29,180 --> 00:01:30,650
Ten dollars there.

28
00:01:30,969 --> 00:01:32,439
Too small to feel.

29
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:34,109
And that's the point.

30
00:01:34,430 --> 00:01:42,090
In that same survey, forty-two percent of people said they'd forgotten they were still paying for something they no longer used.

31
00:01:42,739 --> 00:01:46,000
The industry even has a word for this kind of money:

32
00:01:46,319 --> 00:01:51,719
breakage. It's revenue from people who pay but don't use what they paid for.

33
00:01:52,040 --> 00:01:54,709
For a business, it's almost pure profit.

34
00:01:55,030 --> 00:01:58,250
A customer who forgets costs nothing to serve.

35
00:01:58,900 --> 00:02:00,670
Engine number three:

36
00:02:00,989 --> 00:02:04,290
friction. Signing up takes one click.

37
00:02:04,609 --> 00:02:06,980
Cancelling can feel like a maze.

38
00:02:07,299 --> 00:02:10,219
Are you sure? Here's a discount.

39
00:02:10,539 --> 00:02:13,419
Tell us why. Are you really sure?

40
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:18,370
Every extra step is another chance for Penny to give up and keep paying.

41
00:02:19,020 --> 00:02:20,969
This isn't just annoying.

42
00:02:21,289 --> 00:02:33,379
In twenty twenty-five, Amazon agreed to pay two and a half billion dollars to settle the Federal Trade Commission's case over how people were signed up for Prime, and how hard it was to leave.

43
00:02:33,699 --> 00:02:39,180
According to the F T C, the cancellation process was nicknamed the Iliad.

44
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:41,569
After a famously long war.

45
00:02:42,219 --> 00:02:44,680
Regulators tried to fix this.

46
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:49,599
In twenty twenty-four, the F T C finalised a click-to-cancel rule:

47
00:02:49,919 --> 00:02:52,400
leaving should be as easy as joining.

48
00:02:52,719 --> 00:02:58,780
But in July twenty twenty-five, a federal appeals court threw the rule out before it took effect.

49
00:02:59,099 --> 00:03:02,259
So for now, the maze is mostly still there.

50
00:03:02,909 --> 00:03:04,610
So here's the machine.

51
00:03:04,930 --> 00:03:07,300
The free trial gets your card.

52
00:03:07,620 --> 00:03:09,860
Forgetting keeps the money coming.

53
00:03:10,180 --> 00:03:13,060
And friction makes leaving feel like work.

54
00:03:13,379 --> 00:03:17,750
Companies make the most from the customers who stopped paying attention.

55
00:03:18,069 --> 00:03:19,569
So pay attention.

56
00:03:19,889 --> 00:03:25,409
One: when you start a free trial, set a reminder for the day before it ends.

57
00:03:25,729 --> 00:03:31,830
Two: once a quarter, go through your bank statement and list every recurring charge.

58
00:03:32,150 --> 00:03:36,610
Three: if you wouldn't sign up for it today, cancel it today.

59
00:03:37,259 --> 00:03:40,080
Subscriptions charge you a little, forever.

60
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:45,969
But there's a new way to buy that splits the price into tiny pieces and calls it free.

61
00:03:46,289 --> 00:03:50,509
How buy now, pay later makes money, next on Money Mechanics.

